177 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 147 0 R
/E (Belgrade consists of 17 city municipalities, 10 urban and 7 suburban, and has approximately 1.7 million residents in its wider administrative area. Its size, diversity, and fast pace can make sustainable living challenging sometimes, but they also offer many opportunities for sustainable travel, responsible consumption, community action, and everyday environmental choices.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
178 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 147 0 R
/E (A particularly positive step towards more sustainable urban mobility is that, since 1 January 2025, regular public transport in Belgrade has been free of charge for residents and visitors. The measure covers city and suburban buses, trams, trolleybuses, and BG Voz trains, making lower-impact travel more accessible to students regardless of their budget. By choosing public transport instead of travelling alone by car whenever possible, students can reduce congestion and emissions while moving around the city more affordably and conveniently. Belgrade is currently the largest European city to have introduced a city-wide free public transport system.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
